# PART 07: The Road to Paris and the Duel of Count d'Albert - From Steel to Devotion
# ==============================================================================
PARTS_06_TO_10[7] = [
    "Traveling north toward the capital along the grand post roads of central France, Julie maintained her male disguise as a handsome, independent young cavalier. Carrying her beloved rapiers and a saddlebag of sheet music and classical scrolls, she navigated the kingdom with total self-assurance. Her path was one of continuous adventure, where her quick wit and master swordsmanship repeatedly proved her finest defense against the hazards of the seventeenth-century road.",
    "In a crowded coaching tavern in the historic city of Villeperdue, near Tours, Julie was taking her evening meal in the common room when a group of young aristocratic officers entered. Among them was the young, hot-tempered Count d'Albert, son of the Duke de Luynes. Misinterpreting Julie's quiet, composed demeanor for insolence, d'Albert made a provocative, insulting remark across the tavern room, demanding immediate satisfaction in the courtyard outside.",
    "Without losing her serene composure, Julie calmly finished her wine, stood up, and followed the young nobleman into the secluded garden behind the tavern. The autumn night was clear and cool, illuminated by the bright, silvery light of a full moon. Stripping off their heavy coats, the two duelists saluted with their rapiers, the slender steel blades whispering through the quiet night air as they crossed en garde on the grass.",
    "Count d'Albert was considered one of the finest young swordsmen in his regiment, attacking with aggressive vigor and textbook precision. Yet Julie anticipated his every movement with effortless ease. Parrying his rapid thrusts with minimal wrist movements, she executed a flawless circular bind, stepped inside his guard, and cleanly ran her blade through his right shoulder, ending the duel in seconds before any fatal harm was done.",
    "As the wounded count sank to one knee, clutching his shoulder in shock and pain, Julie immediately dropped her weapon and rushed forward to assist him. Tearing her own clean linen shirt into bandages, she expertly dressed his wound and helped his companions carry him upstairs to a private bedchamber. It was at this moment, as she leaned over him to tend his injury, that Count d'Albert realized his vanquisher was an extraordinarily beautiful young woman.",
    "Astonished and deeply humbled by her supreme martial mastery and compassionate care, Count d'Albert was entirely captivated. Over the following days, Julie remained at the inn, nursing the young officer back to health with herbal teas, clean dressings, and lively, witty conversation. What began as a duel of honor rapidly blossomed into a profound, passionate romance and an enduring bond of mutual devotion that would last their entire lives.",
    "When d'Albert was sufficiently recovered to rejoin his military regiment, the two parted with heartfelt affection and mutual promises of lifelong loyalty. Count d'Albert vowed to champion Julie's cause wherever his travels took him, declaring that she possessed the bravest heart and the noblest soul in all of France. Their friendship would remain a constant, comforting pillar throughout Julie's life in Paris.",
    "Continuing her northward journey, Julie met another celebrated traveler along the Loire valley: the charismatic actor, singer, and theater director Gabriel-Vincent Thévenard. Recognizing each other as kindred artistic spirits, they traveled together toward Paris, practicing operatic duets and theatrical scenes in their carriage as the picturesque châteaux of the Loire glided past like fairy-tale fortresses in the afternoon light.",
    "Thévenard, who possessed a magnificent baritone voice and was destined to become one of the premier stars of the Paris Opera, was awe-struck by the depth and flexibility of Julie's contralto. He encouraged her to seek an immediate audition with the Royal Academy of Music, assuring her that the Parisian public would embrace her as a sensation unlike anything the stage had ever witnessed in France.",
    "As the silhouette of Paris appeared on the horizon, bathed in the soft golden haze of late afternoon, Julie felt a surge of joyful anticipation. She was returning to the city of her youth not as a frightened fugitive, but as an accomplished artist and master of the blade, ready to conquer the grandest stage in Europe. Taking a slow, deep breath, she rode through the city gates into the dawn of her greatest triumph."
]

